.page nc 1 connect to a TCP or UDP port, or listen on one

# SYNOPSIS
*nc* [*-uvz*] [*-w* _SECONDS_] _HOST_ _PORT_
*nc* *-l* [*-u*] [*-v*] [*-w* _SECONDS_] [*-p* _PORT_ | _PORT_] [_ADDRESS_]

# DESCRIPTION
`nc` ("netcat") connects standard input and standard output to a network connection. In the first form it connects to _HOST_ (a name or dotted address) and _PORT_ and copies in both directions: what you type or pipe in is sent, what arrives is printed. When standard input ends, the sending direction of the connection is closed (a TCP half-close) and `nc` keeps printing until the other side hangs up; with `-w` it also gives up after that many silent seconds. This is enough to talk to a web server by hand or to send a file through a pipe.

With `-l` it waits for one incoming connection (or, with `-u`, for the first datagram, whose sender becomes the peer), then relays in the same way and exits. Ports below 1024 need root.

# OPTIONS
@ *-u*
    UDP instead of TCP.
@ *-l*
    Listen instead of connecting.
@ *-p* _PORT_
    The port to listen on (or give it as the last argument).
@ *-z*
    Only check whether the port accepts a connection; send nothing. Exit 0 if it does.
@ *-v*
    Say what happens (listening, connected, who connected).
@ *-w* _SECONDS_
    For connecting: give up after this long. For relaying: leave after this many silent seconds once standard input has ended.

# EXAMPLES
    printf 'GET / HTTP/1.0\r\n\r\n' | nc 10.0.2.2 8000
    nc -l -p 9000 > received.bin
    nc -z -v 10.0.2.2 80
    nc -u -l 5353

# EXIT STATUS
0 when the connection (or the check) worked and ended normally, 1 on errors and refusals, 2 for a usage error.

# SEE ALSO
fetch(1), httpd(1), network(7)
